WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:05.638
The writer HG Wells, who
first imagined time machines

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:10.009
and alien invasions, had a
nightmare of a future world

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.312
where atoms were weaponized.

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:16.282
In his book called "The World
Set Free", written in 1913,

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.718
he coined the
phrase atomic bombs

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:22.856
and loosed them on helpless
civilian populations.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:27.427
He set his vision of a nuclear
war between England and Germany

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.730
in the impossibly distant
future of the 1950s.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:33.533
[music playing]

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.883
[bomb exploding]

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:03.430
In 1933, the Hungarian
physicist, Leo Szilard,

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:07.967
was contemplating
becoming a biologist.

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:09.869
Dr. Szilard?

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.738
Are you quite all
right in there?

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.107
NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON:
He read Wells's novel,

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:15.475
and it started him thinking.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:26.085
Szilard knew that atoms are
made of protons and neutrons

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:28.721
on the inside and
a skittering vale

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:31.057
of electrons on the outside.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.126
Suddenly, while
waiting for the light

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:35.762
to change at this
intersection in London,

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:39.933
he was struck by the thought,
if he could find a sufficiently

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.269
large amount of an element
that would emit two neutrons

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:46.739
when it absorbed
one, it would sustain

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:49.542
a nuclear chain reaction.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:52.979
Two would produce four, four
would produce eight, and so

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:57.317
forth, until enormous amounts
of energy in the nucleus

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.286
itself could be liberated.

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:03.590
Not a chemical reaction,
but a nuclear one.

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:06.192
[alarm wailing]

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.229
[bomb exploding]

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:12.298
[music playing]

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:18.771
This was the moment
our world changed.

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:26.779
Leo Szilard also knew the
power of exponentials,

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:30.083
and if a neutron chain reaction
could be triggered down there

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.285
in the world of
the atom's nucleus,

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:34.854
then something like Wells's
imaginary atomic bomb

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.422
might be possible.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:40.159
He shuddered at the thought of
this destructive capability.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.995
It was just the
latest development

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:46.533
on a continuum of violence
that began long, long before.

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:55.775
50,000 years ago, all
humans were roving

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.378
bands of hunter-gatherers.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.847
They communicated
over limited areas

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:04.817
by calling to one another,
that is, at the speed of sound,

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:09.923
around 750 miles per hour,
but over longer distances,

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:13.560
they could communicate only
as fast as they could run.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:17.130
Around 12,000 years ago, about
the same time as the invention

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.499
of agriculture, they
developed the power

00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:22.235
to kill at a longer distance.

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:25.572
The kill radius expanded
to the arc of an arrow

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:28.274
launched by a bow,
and they could kill

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:31.311
one person with a single arrow.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:34.280
Our ancestors were not
particularly warlike

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:36.583
because there were so few
people and so much room

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:40.853
back then that moving on was
preferable to armed conflict.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.457
Their weapons were used
almost entirely for hunting.

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:47.493
Their identification
horizon was likely small,

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.031
only with the other members of
their band of 50 or 100 people.

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:55.702
But their time horizon
took a giant leap.

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:02.342
They worked long and
hard planting crops

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.877
in the here and now,
so several months

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:07.347
later, they could harvest them.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.816
They postponed
present gratification

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:13.319
for later advantage.

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:17.290
They began to plan
for the future.

